@roidelapluie thanks for the link - unfortunately I think it really does not answer most of my questions, especially those on the last line of my last comment. I see you validate the input duration like:

```go
func validateBlockDuration(t int64) bool {
	i, f := math.Modf(float64(t) / float64(tsdb.DefaultBlockDuration))
	if f != 0 {
		return false
	}

	i, f = math.Modf(math.Log2(i))
	return f == 0 && i >= 0
}
```

I have a couple questions:

1. why are you using floating point arithmetic here when the inputs are integer numbers of nanoseconds (`time.Duration`)?
2. am I understanding this correctly in that the only valid block sizes are powers of 2 multiples of the base duration?
3. if so, why even expose a duration flag to the users when there's such a narrow range of possible values it can take? why not expose e.g. the integer we'll exponentiate 2 by? For instance `--block-duration-factor 3` would mean a duration of `2 ** 3 * 2h = 16h`

@brian-brazil thanks for the input! Some follow up questions:

- As I understand it, you're saying that other projects / derivative users of TSDB like Thanos are not using the same default block size, so we *do* want the backfill tool here to allow a user to configure their own block size, since it's important for the size to a) be some exponentiated multiple of 2 of the default block size for the TSDB they are filling and b) for the blocks to be aligned, but it's not possible to know what the default is or what the alignment should be for any generic TSDB? Or are there utilities once connected to the TSDB that would allow us to validate at runtime that the passed duration is both a correct multiple and to internally align it to the right boundary?
- Why is a mega block not desirable? I'd love to add documentation to this feature as well so that users can choose the correct options. For me (and I'd guess for many others) the backfill tool is extremely useful when writing end-to-end tests of some derivative software that needs to contact Prometheus via the API. In these cases, we can be certain of a number of things - for instance, we know that there (may not be) any existing data in the TSDB we're inserting into, or that we will never add new data that would overlap with old data. I'd love to write guidance to users of the backfill tool to help them understand in which cases they might want to just use one big block, when it's appropriate, and when they just need to eat the insertion cost of many smaller blocks instead.
